    "math_academy_id": "q-109090",
    "knowledge_point_id": "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable",
    "problem": "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |",
    "worked_solution": "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
